 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**The 10-year bond yield is currently about 0.95 percentage points above the cash rate.**\n\n- **10-year government bond:** 5.31% on 14 October 2026 (latest daily reading).\n- **Cash rate target:** 4.36% (September 2026 monthly average, the latest published). The RBA's October 2026 monthly figure isn't in the data yet, so the gap uses September's rate.\n\n**How the gap has moved:**\n\n| Period | Cash rate | 10-year yield | Gap (approx.) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Jan 2025 | 4.35% | ~4.48% | +0.1 pt |\n| Jun–Jul 2025 | 3.85% | ~4.2–4.3% | +0.4 pt |\n| Jan 2026 | 3.60% | ~4.7% | +1.1 pts |\n| Sep 2026 | 4.36% | ~5.2–5.3% | +0.9 pt |\n| 14 Oct 2026 | 4.36% (Sep avg) | 5.31% | +0.95 pt |\n\nThe cash rate has risen about 0.76 points since January 2026, while the 10-year has risen about 0.6 points. The gap was widest in early 2026, when the market priced in a lower policy rate than the bond yield implied, and has narrowed as the RBA lifted the cash rate. The 10-year is now at its highest level in this series since at least 2022, a sign that longer-term borrowing costs are rising faster than the policy rate.\n\nA 10-year yield above the cash rate is the normal shape. It means investors want extra return for locking money up for a decade, and it suggests they expect rates to stay higher for longer. A yield below the cash rate (an inverted curve) would signal expected rate cuts.",
